	<h1>Work</h1>

	<section id="improv">
		<h2>Improv theatre</h2>
		<p>
			I've been improvising since 2011, mostly out of Antwerp. I co-founded Swaajp Improtheater and
			later its improv school, and I currently perform with three ensembles: Commotie, Prism, and
			Werewolves. Between them they've won Best Longform Improv Show in Flanders (Commotie, 2017)
			and the Kemphanen Flemish improv championship (Prism, among others in 2019). I've performed
			more than 250 shows in ten countries and four languages, competed at the 2024 European
			Championships for Improvisational Theatre, and helped found The SIN, a network linking
			improvisers across Europe. I'll usually take a chance to play percussion from the side of the
			stage as well.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="science-communication">
		<h2>Science communication — ERLNMYR</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="https://www.erlnmyr.be" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ERLNMYR</a> is the
			company I founded to treat science communication as theatre rather than a one-way lecture. It
			covers four things: training, consultation, hosting events, and full science-theatre
			performances, each tailored to its audience — from academic conferences down to a classroom
			of ten-year-olds. The work has picked up some recognition: third place at FameLab Belgium, the
			KVAB Annual Science Communication Prize in 2017, and a performance at Bright Club Brussels.
			More recently I've added workshops on AI in science communication — using it as a subject to
			explain, a tool to prepare with, and occasionally a creative partner on stage.
		</p>
		<p><a class="button" href="https://www.erlnmyr.be" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book a show or workshop</a></p>
	</section>

	<section id="teaching">
		<h2>Teaching</h2>
		<p>
			Alongside performing, I teach improv at the Swaajp Improschool in Antwerp and at the Mechelen
			Conservatory, plus workshops internationally. My approach leans on a calm presence, close
			attention to the group in front of me, and always looking for both fun and meaning in a
			scene — the same instinct that drives the ERLNMYR training and coaching work.
		</p>
	</section>
